### What is decomposition by business capability?

Each service owns a capability the business offers. You list what the bank does and each becomes a service.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Each service owns one business capability end to end.

// PaymentService: everything about payments
public class PaymentService {
    public void initiateTransfer(String fromId, String toId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        // Handles transfer initiation, settlement, reversal
    }
}

// LoanService: everything about loans
public class LoanService {
    public void sanctionLoan(String customerId, java.math.BigDecimal principal) {
        // Handles sanction, EMI calculation, disbursement
    }
}

// NotificationService: everything about notifications
public class NotificationService {
    public void sendSms(String customerId, String message) {
        // Handles SMS, email, push
    }
}
```

```text
Boundary is stable because business capabilities change slowly.
Ownership is clear: one team owns "payments" end to end.

WRONG: Split by technical layer (ControllerService, DAOService)
  -> Every feature spans every service. Distributed monolith.
```

### What is decomposition by DDD subdomain?

A bounded context is a region where a term has one precise meaning. Each bounded context becomes a service with its own model.

```java
package com.bank.lending.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// In the Lending context, "Account" means a loan account.
public class LoanAccount {
    private String loanAccountId;
    private BigDecimal principal;
    private BigDecimal interestRate;
    private int tenureMonths;
    // This is NOT the same as a deposit account.
}
```

```java
package com.bank.deposits.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// In the Deposits context, "Account" means a savings/current account.
public class DepositAccount {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private String accountType; // SAV, CUR
    // This is NOT the same as a loan account.
}
```

```text
"Account" means different things in different contexts:
  Deposits: savings account with a balance
  Lending: loan account with principal and EMI
  Cards: credit card account with a limit

Each context has its own model. No shared "Account" class.
```

### What is decomposition by transaction?

Keep operations that must be atomic together in one service. If two pieces of data must always change together, splitting them forces a Saga.

```java
package com.bank.ledger;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class LedgerService {

    @Transactional
    public void postEntry(String accountId, BigDecimal debit, BigDecimal credit) {
        // Debit and credit of a single ledger posting MUST be atomic.
        // Keep them in ONE service, ONE transaction.
        // Splitting them would force a Saga for something that should be simple.
    }
}
```

```text
Rule: Keep strongly consistent operations in one service.
Accept Sagas only across boundaries where eventual consistency is fine.

Keep together: debit + credit of a single ledger posting
Split with Saga: debit Rajesh (Account Service) + credit Priya (Account Service)
  -> Two different accounts, two different aggregates, Saga is appropriate.
```

### What is decomposition by scalability?

Split out a component whose load profile differs sharply from the rest. If fraud scoring needs 10x CPU at peak, extract it.

```text
Load profiles at National Bank of India:

  Component        | Normal Load  | Peak Load   | Scale Factor
  ─────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼─────────────
  Account queries   | 1000 req/s  | 2000 req/s  | 2x
  Payment transfers | 500 req/s   | 5000 req/s  | 10x
  Fraud scoring    | 500 req/s   | 5000 req/s  | 10x
  Notifications    | 200 req/s   | 200 req/s   | 1x

Payment and Fraud need 10x scaling at festival peak.
Notifications barely change.
Extract Payment and Fraud so they scale without dragging Notifications.
```

### How do the four strategies combine?

In practice you combine all four. Start with business capabilities, refine with DDD subdomains, keep strongly consistent operations together, and carve out scalability hotspots.

```text
Combined decomposition:
  1. List business capabilities -> Account, Payment, Loan, Notification, Fraud
  2. Refine with DDD -> "Account" means deposit in Deposits context,
     loan in Lending context
  3. Transaction check -> debit+credit of one posting stays in one service
  4. Scalability check -> Fraud scoring extracted (10x peak scaling needed)
```

### How do you test if a boundary is correct?

The test: a well-decomposed service changes for one reason and talks to others rarely. If it changes whenever anything changes and chatters constantly, the cut was wrong.

```text
GOOD boundary:
  Payment Service changes only when payment logic changes.
  Talks to Account Service once per transfer.
  Can be deployed independently.

BAD boundary:
  PaymentController Service changes with every feature.
  Calls BusinessLogic Service and DAOService for everything.
  Must deploy with both. Distributed monolith.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is decomposing by technical layer instead of business capability.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Technical layer decomposition.
// Every feature spans all services.
public class WrongDecomposition {
    // api-gateway-service: routes
    // business-logic-service: rules
    // database-service: persistence
    // Result: distributed monolith.
}

// CORRECT: Business capability decomposition.
public class CorrectDecomposition {
    // payment-service: owns payment logic + payment DB
    // account-service: owns account logic + account DB
    // Each service is self-contained.
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The goal is high cohesion and low coupling. A well-decomposed service changes for one reason and talks to others rarely. If it changes whenever anything changes, redraw the boundary.
